course: world
lesson: 116
title: How our calendar was made: Romans, Julius Caesar and Pope Gregory
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A calendar is a set of rules for counting days into months and years so that the dates stay in step with the seasons. That is harder than it sounds, because the Earth takes about 365 days and 6 hours (a tiny bit less) to go round the Sun, not a neat number of days.
explain: The oldest Roman calendar started the year in March, so September ("septem" is 7 in Latin) really was the 7th month. When January and February were moved to the front, the old names stayed behind, like a kid who keeps the nickname "Shorty" after growing tall.
explain: Before Julius Caesar, Roman priests added extra days whenever they felt like it, sometimes to keep their friends in power longer, and the calendar drifted months away from the seasons. In 46 BC Caesar made one year 445 days long to catch up, and from 45 BC his Julian calendar had 365 days with a leap day every 4 years.
explain: Caesar's year was about 11 minutes too long. Tiny, but after about 1,600 years the calendar had slipped 10 days, so in 1582 Pope Gregory XIII cut 10 dates out once and changed the rule: a century year is a leap year only if it divides by 400. This Gregorian calendar is the one most of the world uses today.
explain: Countries switched at different times: Britain in 1752, Russia and Ukraine in 1918. So old dates can come in two versions, "old style" and "new style", and a historian must always say which calendar a date is in.
fact: The oldest Roman calendar began the year in March, so September, October, November and December were months 7, 8, 9 and 10.
fact: In Latin, septem means 7, octo means 8, novem means 9 and decem means 10.
fact: July is named after Julius Caesar, and August after Augustus, the first Roman emperor.
fact: In 46 BC Julius Caesar made one year 445 days long, 80 days more than 365, to put the calendar back in step with the seasons; it was called the year of confusion.
fact: Caesar's Julian calendar, from 45 BC, had 365 days and a leap day every 4 years, so its average year was 365 days and 6 hours.
fact: The Julian year was about 11 minutes too long, which adds up to about one whole day every 130 years (11 × 130 = 1,430 minutes, close to the 1,440 minutes in a day).
fact: In 1582 Pope Gregory XIII brought in the Gregorian calendar: Thursday 4 October was followed by Friday 15 October, skipping 10 dates.
fact: In the Gregorian calendar a century year is a leap year only if it divides exactly by 400, so 2000 was a leap year but 1900 was not.
fact: Britain switched in 1752, when 2 September was followed by 14 September, skipping 11 dates.
fact: Historians think the famous riots of people shouting "give us our eleven days" probably never happened.
fact: Russia and Ukraine switched in 1918, when the gap had grown to 13 days (it had been 13 days since 1900), so old papers may show dates as "O.S." (old style, Julian) and "N.S." (new style, Gregorian).
fact: The Ethiopian calendar has 12 months of 30 days and a short 13th month of 5 or 6 days; its year number is about 7 or 8 behind ours and its New Year is in September.
fact: Shakespeare and Cervantes both died on "23 April 1616", but England used the Julian calendar and Spain the Gregorian, so they died about 10 days apart.
exercise: Why is September named after the number 7 when it is the 9th month? | The oldest Roman year began in March, when September was month 7 | The Romans counted the months backwards from December | The French moved September two places later in the year | September was named after seven important Roman gods
exercise: What does "decem", in the name December, mean in Latin? | 10 | 12 | 7 | 2
exercise: Who is the month of July named after? | Julius Caesar | Augustus | Pope Gregory | A Roman sun god
exercise: Who is the month of August named after? | Augustus, the first Roman emperor | Julius Caesar, who fixed the calendar | Pope Gregory, who made the new calendar | A Roman goddess of the summer harvest
exercise: What went wrong with the Roman calendar before Julius Caesar? | Priests added days when they liked, so it drifted from the seasons | It had no months at all, only a long list of weeks | It had a leap day every single year without fail | Nobody in Rome was allowed to know the date
exercise: How many days long was the "year of confusion", 46 BC? | 445 | 365 | 366 | 400
exercise: How many more days than 365 did that year have? | 80 | 45 | 10 | 11
exercise: What was the Julian calendar's leap-year rule? | A leap day every 4 years, with no exceptions | A whole leap day added only every hundred years | A whole leap month slipped in every three years | No leap days at all, just 365 days in every year
exercise: How wrong was the Julian year? | About 11 minutes too long | About 11 whole days too short | About 6 whole hours too long | Not wrong at all, it was perfect
exercise: About how often did that error add up to a whole day? | About every 130 years | Every 4 years | Every single year | About every 1,000 years
exercise: In 1582, what day came straight after Thursday 4 October? | Friday 15 October | Friday 5 October | Thursday 14 October | Monday 15 October
exercise: Which of these century years was a leap year? | 2000 | 1900 | 1800 | 1700
exercise: When did Britain switch to the Gregorian calendar? | 1752 | 1582 | 1918 | 1066
exercise: How many dates did Britain skip in September 1752? | 11 | 10 | 13 | 12
exercise: What do historians think about the "give us our eleven days" riots? | They probably never happened | They happened in every British town | They made Britain switch back | They lasted eleven whole days
exercise: On old Ukrainian papers, what does "O.S." next to a date mean? | Old style, a date in the Julian calendar | Official stamp, put on by the post office | Ordinary Sunday, a day with no work | Only summer, a date in the warm months
exercise: How many months does the Ethiopian calendar have? | 13 | 12 | 10 | 14
exercise: Why did Shakespeare and Cervantes not really die on the same day? | England and Spain used different calendars then | One of them really died in a different year | The dates were changed by mistake in books | Spain was in a different time zone from England
test: Ollie goes, "sept means 7 innit, so September's month 7, end of." His sister: "It's month 9, ya plonker." (A plonker is a silly person.) What's the full story? | It's month 9 now, but it was month 7 when the Roman year began in March | Ollie's right, September is still the 7th month of the year | His sister's right, and "sept" has never meant 7 | The French moved September two months later on purpose, after their revolution
test: Mia: "Two Roman blokes got their own months? Lowkey swag." ("Lowkey" means a bit; "swag" means cool.) Which two? | Julius Caesar got July and Augustus got August | Augustus got June and Caesar got the month of July | Caesar got March and Augustus got the month of May | Romulus got July and his brother Remus got August
test: Zara: "445 days in one year? Bloody hell, imagine the homework." Why was 46 BC so long? | Caesar added days to catch up with the seasons before his new calendar | The Romans counted two winters that year by mistake | The Earth went round the Sun more slowly that year | Priests wanted a longer school year for children
test: What would happen if we had kept Caesar's calendar, unchanged, for another 1,300 years? | It would slip about 10 more days away from the seasons | Nothing, because Caesar's year was exactly right | It would slip by only about 10 minutes in all | Christmas would land right in the middle of a hot summer
test: Maya, from Cork: "Bed on Thursday the 4th, up on Friday the 15th? Yer man the Pope's an eejit, they lost ten days of their lives!" ("Yer man" means that fella; "eejit" means idiot.) What's true? | Nobody lost any time; only the date numbers jumped | They really slept for ten whole days without waking up once | Everyone got ten days older overnight, all at once | The weekdays jumped too, so Friday came twice that week
test: Jack's dad: "In 1752 folk rioted, yelling 'give us our eleven days!' Fact." What would a careful historian say? | The riots probably never happened; the story is likely a myth | It's true, and the angry riots won the eleven days back again | It's true, but it happened in Kyiv in 1918 | It's true, and the king gave back five of the days
test: Taras's great-grandad's papers say "1 January 1910 O.S." Mum goes, "Sound, that's 14 January our way." ("Sound" means fine.) Is she right? | Yes, by then the gap between the two calendars was 13 days | No, the gap between the calendars was always 10 days | No, "O.S." means the date is one whole year earlier | Yes, because January had 14 extra days in the old calendar
test: Abel's mate from the Valleys: "13 months? I'd be tamping, that's an extra month of school!" ("Tamping" is Welsh slang for furious.) What does the Ethiopian calendar really have? | 12 months of 30 days and a short 13th month of 5 or 6 days | 13 months of 28 days each, every single year | 13 months of 30 days, making 390 days a year | 12 months and a spare week added in summer
test: Leon: "So my great-great-whatever's birthday from 1500 is wrong now?" What's the best answer? | The day was real, but you must say which calendar the date is in | Yes, every date written before 1582 is fake | No, both calendars always gave the same date | Yes, everyone's birthday moved ten days later, so it's all wrong
test: A cartoon says Pope Gregory invented leap years. Put it right. | Caesar's calendar already had them; Gregory changed the rule for century years | Leap years were invented in 1752 by the British king | Gregory got rid of leap years altogether | Leap years were invented in Ukraine in 1918
test: Gran: "2000 wasn't a leap year, love, century years never are." Is she right? | No, 2000 was a leap year because it divides by 400 | Yes, no century year can ever be a leap year, not one | Yes, only years ending in 4 are leap years | No, every century year is a leap year
test: Ben's teacher calls the old Roman calendar "a right dog's dinner" (a big mess). Why didn't it fit the seasons? | Priests added days when they felt like it, sometimes for politics | It had a leap day every 4 years with no exceptions | The Romans had no words for summer and winter | It followed the Sun far too closely
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the month designated September bears a name signifying "seven". | The Roman year originally commenced in March, rendering September the seventh month | September was originally the seventh month in the calendar of the French | The month was dedicated to seven deities of the Roman state | The name was assigned in error by Pope Gregory the Thirteenth
exam: Given that the Julian year exceeded the solar year by approximately eleven minutes, ascertain the approximate interval over which the error accumulated to one entire day. | Approximately one hundred and thirty years | Approximately four years | Approximately eleven years | Approximately one thousand six hundred years
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the reform promulgated in 1582. | Ten dates were omitted, and century years became leap years only if divisible by 400 | Eleven dates were omitted, and leap years were abolished entirely | Ten dates were added, and every year became 366 days | The year was thereafter commenced in the month of March
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the adoption of the Gregorian calendar in Britain? | It occurred in 1752, whereupon 2 September was followed by 14 September | It occurred in 1582, at which time 4 October was followed by 15 October | It occurred in 1918, whereupon thirteen dates were omitted | It has not yet occurred, Britain retaining the Julian calendar
